"Represents hierarchical composition with aligned rectangular bands whose widths encode subtree totals at successive depths.\n\nUse when: Readers need to trace ancestry while comparing hierarchical shares on a linear scale. A hierarchy has enough width for useful labels in its larger branches.\n\nAvoid when: Exact leaf values are more important than hierarchy or depth is excessive.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal span encodes the additive value of a subtree. Vertical band encodes hierarchy depth; alignment encodes ancestry.\n\nBuild: Validate parent-child structure and sum leaf values upward. Partition each parent’s horizontal span among its children proportionally. Use constant depth-band heights and reserved text padding inside sufficiently wide rectangles.\n\nCheck: Check child widths sum to their parent width. Verify sibling ordering remains stable across comparison panels. Confirm parent values are not added again on top of descendant totals.\n\nPitfalls: A tall hierarchy can consume substantial space even when few branches matter. Tiny labels should move to a detail list rather than crossing rectangle borders.\n\nExample: Show synthetic product spending by portfolio, product, and feature with each descendant directly below its parent span.\n\nAlternatives: Sunburst chart, Treemap, Circle-packing chart."
